 "differences": [
  {
   "sanskrit": 23,
   "text": "/udbhāvanatā/ („bringing to light, making appear“) is rendered /brjod pa/ („stating, expressing“), /upādāya/ („on account of“) as /phyir/ („because“); the abstract -tā is not rendered.  The Tibetan has the skandhas state the self-basis rather than bring it forth."
  },
  {
   "sanskrit": 24,
   "text": "/parigraha/ is rendered /yongs su gzung ba/ („what is fully grasped, appropriated“) — the body with what it has appropriated, not its outward possessions."
  },
  {
   "sanskrit": 27,
   "text": "The dvandva is spelled out with /dang/; /abhisaṃskāra/ becomes the verbal phrase /mngon par 'du byed pa/ („forming, compounding“)."
  },
  {
   "sanskrit": 28,
   "text": "/tad-/ is read as plural, /de dag/ („those“); /phyir ro/ closes the answer that /phyir te/ opened in 78."
  }
 ],
 "rendering": "The Tibetan keeps the structure: /zhe na/ marks the question, /phyir te/ opens and /phyir ro/ closes the answer, /dang/ joins the five modes.  It renders the key terms consistently — /ātmavastu/ as /bdag gi gzhi/ („basis of self“), /udbhāvanatām upādāya/ as /brjod pa'i phyir/ („because of stating“).  That last choice shifts the sense: in the Sanskrit the skandhas bring the self-basis to light (/udbhāvanā/, from the causative of ud-√bhū); in the Tibetan they state or express it (/brjod pa/) — the translators read /udbhāvana/ in its sense „making known, declaring“."
}